\section*{Abstract}
This project aims to design and implement a pipelined processor called 32-bit mini-MIPS similar to RISC-V. Three instruction formats, including R, I, and J were considered, supporting eleven instructions. The processor was designed using VHDL and implemented with a five-stage pipeline. To prevent hazards, a hazard detection unit and forwarding unit were included. 
\section{Introduction} \label{intro} %Nitish
The objective of this project is to design and build a simple pipelined processor called mini-MIPS, which is a simplified version of the MIPS (Microprocessor without Interlocked Pipeline Stages) with an instruction set architecture of 32-bit format. The mini-MIPS processor is related to the RISC (Reduced Instruction Set Computer) which has a fixed number of address identifiers which is highly sought after because of its better performance and high energy efficiency. 

For our implementation of the mini-MIPS, we consider three types of instruction formats namely, “R”, “I”, and “J” types, that supports the operations of eleven instructions for this project namely, “NOR”, “SLL”, “XOR”, “ANDI”, “SUBUI”, “ADD”, “BEQ”, “LW”, “SB”, “JR” and “J”. The mini-MIPS processor was implemented using VHDL with three instruction formats and eleven instructions. It was simulated, compiled, and executed using ModelSim and Microsoft Visual Studio Code. The microprocessor was designed into a five stage pipeline involving stages namely Instruction Fetch (IF), Instruction Decode (ID), Execution (EX), Data Memory (MEM) and Write Back (WB), also we have introduced pipeline buffers between intermediate stages to ease the data transfer between them. To avoid potential hazards such as data hazards and control hazards in the pipeline we have included a Hazard Detection unit in the ID Stage, that handles the control hazards and a Forwarding Unit in the EX stage, that handles forwarding of operand data from a previous instruction to overcome data hazards by resolving data dependencies in the pipelined execution of multiple instructions.


The report is organized in several sections covering the design and implementation of a computer processor. The first section covers the instruction set architecture design and encoding. The second section covers the datapath design including the components of the datapath. The third section covers the design of the control unit. The fourth section covers the design of the hazard control unit. The fifth section covers the design of the forwarding unit. Finally, the sixth section covers simulation and testing results. The report concludes with a summary of the processor's design and potential areas for future improvement.


\section{Instruction Set Architecture Design and Encoding} \label{sota}
\subsection{MIPS Instruction formats} %Nitish
As mentioned before, the implementation of our MIPS processor is based on four instruction formats namely, the R-type, I-type and J-type. These formats define how an instruction is supposed to be decoded and the type of operation to be performed on the given constraint by the microprocessor. So, let us discuss about the instruction formats in more detail to understand their functionality in the processor.
\subsubsection{R-Type Instructions} %Nour
R-type instructions are primarily designed for register-based operations. These instructions format is 32-bits long and consists of six individual components. These components include the operational code (OPCODE) which is a 6-bit field, the source register (Rs) with a 5-bit field, target register (Rt) with a 5-bit field, destination register (Rd) with a 5-bit field, shift amount (Shamt) with a 5-bit field, and function with a 6-bit field. In R-type instruction format, the OPCODE is always zero (except  for JR), the Shamt field is used for shifting data, and the function field is used to declare the specific function to be performed. 

Our processor supports five R-type instruction (e.g. ADD, NOR,  XOR, SLL, and JR). ADD, NOR, and XOR involve the opcode, source register operands (rs and rt), destination register operand (rd), shift amount, and function code. ADD performs addition of two register operands, NOR performs a bitwise logical NOR of two register operands, and XOR performs a bitwise logical exclusive OR of two register operands. SLL involves the opcode, source register operand (rt), destination register operand (rd), shift amount, and function code, and it shifts a register operand left by a number of bits specified by the shift amount. JR, on the other hand, only involves the opcode and register operand (rs) and performs an unconditional jump to the address stored in a register operand. The table below shows encoding examples of each of the instructions mentioned.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
Instruction & Opcode & Rs & Rt & Rd & Shamt & Function \\
 & [31:26] & [25:21] & [20:16] & [15:11] & [10:6] & [5:0] \\
\hline
ADD \$t0, \$t1, \$t2 & 000000 & 01001 & 01010 & 01000 & 00000 & 100000 \\
\hline
NOR \$t0, \$t1, \$t2 & 000000 & 01001 & 01010 & 01000 & 00000 & 100111 \\
\hline
XOR \$t0, \$t1, \$t2 & 000000 & 01001 & 01010 & 01000 & 00000 & 100110 \\
\hline
SLL \$t0, \$t1, 4 & 000000 & 00000 & 01001 & 01000 & 4 & 000000 \\
\hline
JR \$ra & 001000 & Rs & 00000 & 00000 & 00000 & 000000 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}

\end{center}




\subsubsection{I-Type Instructions} 

I-instructions, also known as Immediate instructions, are a type of instruction in MIPS that involve immediate values. Immediate values are 16-bit values that are included in the instruction itself, rather than being stored in a register or memory location. The I-instruction format is also 32-bits long and consists of six individual components: OPCODE, Rs, Rt, Immediate value, and Function.
In I-instructions, the OPCODE field is used to specify the operation to be performed, and the Rs and Rt fields are used to specify the source and destination registers. The Immediate value field is used to specify the immediate value to be used in the operation.
Our project involves implementing a set of I-instructions, including ADDI, SUBUI, SB, LW, and BEQ.

The ANDI instruction (AND Immediate) performs a bitwise AND between the contents of register "rs" and a sign-extended 16-bit immediate value, storing the result in register "rt".
The SUBUI instruction subtracts an immediate value from a register and stores the result in a register. The LW instruction loads a word from memory into a register, and the SB instruction stores a byte from a register into memory. Lastly, the BEQ instruction checks whether the contents of two registers are equal, and if they are, it branches to a specified instruction.


